**Definition 1.3 (all correlated competitors and pressure).** A nonempty
$B\subseteq S_d^n$ is admissible when, for every $(i,q)$, its target
$g_q(x_i)$ is constant on $B$, or there are $j,a$ and a total reply
decoder $\delta$ such that

$$
g_q(x_i)=\delta(r_a(x_j))\qquad(x\in B).
\tag{RCE.6}
$$

The witnesses may depend on $B,i,q$, may query any coordinate, and may
form any cycles. All equations hold on the same tuples. Complete
templates select one equation for every target; their solution sets
are $C_\Theta$. The same-witness saturation of [NDR, Theorem 2.2]
identifies nonnegative mass maxima over admissible sets and complete
templates. For $w:S_d\to[0,\infty)$ set

$$
A_n^{(d)}(w)=\max_{B\text{ admissible}}
       \sum_{x\in B}\prod_{i=1}^nw(x_i),\qquad
\beta_d(w)=\sup_{n\ge1}A_n^{(d)}(w)^{1/n}.
\tag{RCE.7}
$$

Every weight is owned once by its original whole block. It can correlate
all constructor coordinates within that block. No factorization of
that weight is assumed. [CBN, Theorems 4.2 and 6.2] give an exact
equality-aware constructor representation of these same weighted sums;
they give no independent copies of a reply and no uniform width bound.

